This implementation will:

1. Send a `timer_tick` event on each timer update with:
   - Formatted time string
   - Milliseconds remaining

2. Send `timer_state_changed` events when the timer:
   - Starts
   - Pauses
   - Stops
   - Completes
   
3. Provide a WebSocket request `get_timer_state` to query the current timer state

One can now use these events in OBS WebSocket clients to:

- Display timer updates in chat
- Trigger scene changes based on specific times
- Control other OBS elements based on timer state
- Build external applications that react to the timer (i.e. displaying the countdown on my Stream Deck)

#### Example usage in an OBS WebSocket client:

```javascript
// Example of listening for timer events
obs.on('Ashmanix.CountdownTimer.timer_tick', data => {
    console.log(`Timer: ${data.time_display}`);
});

obs.on('Ashmanix.CountdownTimer.timer_state_changed', data => {
    console.log(`Timer state changed to: ${data.state}`);
});
```

